      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gardner was founded in 1904 along a stage line that ran from Westcliffe to Walsenburg, right beneath Gardner Butte where Muddy Creek meets the Huerfano River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.huerfano.org/discover-gardner",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors William Gardner, a local rancher and businessman who helped shape the area in the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://colorado.wiki/a/Gardner,_Colorado",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Gardner’s early economy was almost entirely driven by coal mining, a boom that defined the town’s growth for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://colorado.wiki/a/Gardner,_Colorado",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The region around Gardner is rich in Native American history, with archaeological sites and cultural landmarks scattered throughout the valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://colorado.wiki/a/Gardner,_Colorado",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Like many Colorado coal towns, Gardner experienced strikes as miners fought for better wages and safer working conditions.",
